## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests real-time data tracking with batched updates and sliding window counts. It evaluates the ability to design a data structure that combines efficient timestamp-based aggregation with per-session lookup while managing amortized cleanup.

## Key Concepts

- Batched online updates with timestamp ordering
- Sliding window counts with expiration
- Two-tier data structures (ordered map + hash map)
- Amortized analysis versus naive recalculation

## Code Example

```javascript
class SessionTracker {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.idToTime = new Map();
    this.timeToCount = new Map();
  }

  processBatch(events, currentTime) {
    for (const { sessionId, time, isNew } of events) {
      if (isNew) {
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      } else {
        const oldTime = this.idToTime.get(sessionId);
        this.timeToCount.set(oldTime, this.timeToCount.get(oldTime) - 1);
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      }
    }
    this.expireOldSessions(currentTime);
  }

  expireOldSessions(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    for (const [time, count] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time < threshold) {
        this.timeToCount.delete(time);
      }
    }
  }

  countActive(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    let count = 0;
    for (const [time, c] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time >= threshold) count += c;
    }
    return count;
  }
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing**: O(1) — just initialize empty maps.

**Memory**: O(S) where S is the number of unique timestamps with active sessions; worst case O(N) for N sessions if all have distinct timestamps.

**Update**: Each event is O(log T) for the ordered map insertion/deletion, where T is the number of unique timestamps. With B events per batch, this is O(B log T) amortized.

**Query**: O(T) in the worst case — must scan all timestamps in the window. Using an ordered map with prefix sum augmentation could reduce this to O(log T).

**Comparison to naive recalculation**: Recalculating from scratch after each batch would require O(N) per query, where N is total active sessions. The two-tier structure provides O(1) lookup per session update and O(T) or O(log T) queries — a significant improvement when batches are processed frequently and queries are intermittent.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about fixed-memory distinct counting in streaming data, a common real-world need for analytics in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for cardinality estimation
- Mergeable sketches across batches
- Relative error ~ 1.04/√m; memory fixed by m

## Code Example
```javascript
function hllAdd(registers, x, hash) {
  const m = registers.length;
  const i = hash & (m - 1);
  const w = hash >>> Math.log2(m);
  const rank = leadingZeros(w) + 1;
  registers[i] = Math.max(registers[i], rank);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose p in a live system with memory constraints?
- How would you explain the trade-offs of using HLL vs a linear-counting approach in presence of enormous M?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis under streaming appends with cache considerations. It evaluates understanding of how to design data structures that balance update frequency, query latency, and memory access patterns.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding windows**: Maintaining a fixed-size view over streaming data
- **Circular buffers**: O(1) rotation without shifting elements
- **Amortized analysis**: Batched updates spread costs over time
- **Cache-friendly layout**: Sequential memory access patterns for spatial locality

## Code Example
```javascript
class StreamingAnalyticsStore {
  constructor(W, kQueries, mKeys) {
    this.W = W;
    this.blocks = [];
    this.head = 0;
    this.size = 0;
    this.windowSum = 0;
    this.queryRanges = kQueries.map(([start, end]) => ({ start, end }));
    this.keySet = new Set(mKeys);
  }

  appendBatch(newBlocks) {
    for (const block of newBlocks) {
      if (this.size === this.W) {
        this.windowSum -= this.blocks[this.head];
      }
      this.blocks[this.head] = block;
      this.windowSum += block;
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
      this.size = Math.min(this.size + 1, this.W);
    }
  }

  rangeSum(queryIndex) {
    const { start, end } = this.queryRanges[queryIndex];
    const windowSize = this.size;
    const startIdx = (this.head - end + this.W) % this.W;
    const endIdx = (this.head - start - 1 + this.W) % this.W;
    let sum = 0;
    let i = startIdx;
    while (i !== (endIdx + 1) % this.W) {
      sum += this.blocks[i];
      i = (i + 1) % this.W;
    }
    return sum;
  }

  hasKey(key) {
    return this.keySet.has(key);
  }
}
```

